{"id": "broad-formal-verification", "question": "Who works on formal verification?", "stratum": "broad", "kind": "exhaustive", "ground_truth": "", "expected_slugs": ["andrew-thomas-walter", "ji-yong-shin", "joshua-gancher", "ryan-doenges", "yibo-zhao"], "source_facts": "QUALIFYING PEOPLE (5), harvested offline from the raw corpus by matching ['formal verification', 'proof assistant'] in ['biography', 'research_interests', 'areas_of_interest', 'labs_and_groups', 'projects']:\n- Andrew Thomas Walter (PhD Student, khoury, slug=andrew-thomas-walter): hnic Institute. Andrew's main field of research is Formal Methods, and he is interested in bringing the capabilities of formal verification to novice programmers not trained in formal methods, \u2026\n- Ji-Yong Shin (Assistant Professor, khoury, slug=ji-yong-shin): Ji-Yong Shin is an assistant professor in the Khoury College of Computer Sciences, based in Boston. Shin researches formal verification methods that can be applied to system designs; he also designs \u2026\n- Joshua Gancher (Assistant Professor, khoury, slug=joshua-gancher): on for our daily activities. He began his graduate studies interested in applied cryptography, but soon discovered that formal verification and mechanized proofs offered a level of correctness and \u2026\n- Ryan Doenges (Distinguished Research Fellow, khoury, slug=ryan-doenges): based in Boston. Doenges joined Khoury College in 2023 to continue his research in programming language semantics and formal verification. In particular, he studies hardware design languages, which \u2026\n- Yibo Zhao (PhD Student, khoury, slug=yibo-zhao): odels to improve certain components of computer systems, such as learned index structures. He is also interested in the formal verification of neural networks. His faculty advisors are Cheng Tan and \u2026", "notes": "5 qualifying people; harvest terms ['formal verification', 'proof assistant']"}
{"id": "blended-cs5700", "question": "Who teaches CS 5700 Fundamentals of Computer Networking, and what does that person research?", "stratum": "blended", "kind": "prose", "ground_truth": "", "expected_slugs": ["alan-mislove", "course-cs5700"], "source_facts": "COURSE: CS 5700 Fundamentals of Computer Networking\nDESCRIPTION: Studies network protocols, focusing on modeling and analysis, and architectures. Introduces modeling concepts, emphasizing queuing theory, including Little\u2019s theorem, M/M/1, M/M/m, M/D/1, and M/G/1 queuing systems. Discusses performance evaluation of computer networks including performance metrics, evaluation tools and methodology, simulation techniques, and limitations. Presents the different harmonizing functions needed for communication and efficient operation of computer networks and \u2026\nTERM: Fall 2026 Semester\nINSTRUCTOR: Alan Mislove\n\nINSTRUCTOR PROFILE:\nNAME: Alan Mislove TITLE: Professor, Senior Associate Dean for Academic Affairs COLLEGE: khoury SLUG: alan-mislove RESEARCH_INTERESTS: [\"Network measurement\", \"Networking\", \"Online social networks\", \"Security and privacy\"] BIOGRAPHY: Alan Mislove is a professor and senior associate dean for academic affairs in the Khoury College of Computer Sciences at Northeastern University, based in Boston. Mislove's research focuses on algorithmic auditing. He develops methodologies and studies the real-world systems that millions of users interact with every day, centering on issues of algorithmic discrimination, fairness, and privacy. His aim is to enable regulators, policymakers, and everyday people to better understand how these systems work, how they are used and abused, and what impacts they are having on users and society at large. Mislove\u2019s research comprises more than 80 peer-reviewed papers, has received over 20,000 \u2026", "notes": "needs courses+people; CS; term Fall 2026 Semester"}
{"id": "blended-cy6760", "question": "Who teaches CY 6760 Wireless and Mobile Systems Security, and what does that person research?", "stratum": "blended", "kind": "prose", "ground_truth": "", "expected_slugs": ["aanjhan-ranganathan", "course-cy6760"], "source_facts": "COURSE: CY 6760 Wireless and Mobile Systems Security\nDESCRIPTION: Focuses on security and privacy of mobile and wireless systems. Provides the foundations to understand the security and privacy threats and defenses in wireless and mobile systems, especially in an era where wireless network functions are implemented in software instead of traditional hardware-based solutions. Covers topics on availability/integrity issues in wireless networks, Wi-Fi and cellular network security, broadcast authentication techniques, jamming and antijamming techniques, etc. \u2026\nTERM: Fall 2026 Semester\nINSTRUCTOR: Aanjhan Ranganathan\n\nINSTRUCTOR PROFILE:\nNAME: Aanjhan Ranganathan TITLE: Associate Professor and Interim Director of BS in Cybersecurity Program COLLEGE: khoury SLUG: aanjhan-ranganathan RESEARCH_INTERESTS: [\"Security and privacy\", \"Autonomous cyber-physical systems\", \"Wireless and mobile systems\", \"Embedded and IoT\"] AREAS_OF_INTEREST: [\"Cybersecurity and Privacy\"] BIOGRAPHY: Aanjhan Ranganathan is an associate professor and the interim director of the BS in Cybersecurity program in the Khoury College of Computer Sciences at Northeastern University. He is interested in building secure autonomous cyber-physical systems and has worked on a wide variety of topics, including physical-layer security of wireless systems, secure localization and proximity verification, trusted computing architectures, and side-channels. He is a recipient of several awards including the outstanding dissertation award from ETH Zurich, regional winner of European Space Agency\u2019s Satellite Navigation competition, and the Cyber Award from Armasuisse (Switzerland\u2019s Department of Defense). \u2026", "notes": "needs courses+people; CY; term Fall 2026 Semester"}
{"id": "blended-ds3050", "question": "Who teaches DS 3050 Information and Uncertainty, and what does that person research?", "stratum": "blended", "kind": "prose", "ground_truth": "", "expected_slugs": ["don-fallis", "course-ds3050"], "source_facts": "COURSE: DS 3050 Information and Uncertainty\nDESCRIPTION: Introduces the foundations of probabilistic inference, information theory, and their uses for drawing conclusions from noisy data. Applications include diagnosing diseases with inconclusive medical tests, locating autonomous vehicles when sensors are imperfect, and how best to make inferences with incomplete or partial information. Central topics include distinguishing deductive and probabilistic inference, philosophical interpretations of probability, fundamental justifications for the rules \u2026\nTERM: Fall 2026 Semester\nINSTRUCTOR: Don Fallis\n\nINSTRUCTOR PROFILE:\nNAME: Don Fallis TITLE: Professor, Jointly Appointed with College of Social Sciences and Humanities COLLEGE: khoury SLUG: don-fallis RESEARCH_INTERESTS: [\"Epistemology\", \"Philosophy of information\", \"Philosophy of mathematics\"] AREAS_OF_INTEREST: [\"Human-Centered Computing\"] BIOGRAPHY: Don Fallis is a professor in the Khoury College of Computer Sciences and the College of Social Sciences and Humanities at Northeastern University, based in Boston. Fallis\u2019 main focus is adversarial epistemology, where he examines how individuals obtain knowledge in a deceptive world while understanding different types of lies and disinformation. He regularly teaches courses on decision-making, information economics, information ethics, information quality, and knowledge in the digital world. Before joining Northeastern University, Fallis was a professor in the School of Information at the University of Arizona. He has also held visiting fellowships at the University of St. Andrews and the \u2026", "notes": "needs courses+people; DS; term Fall 2026 Semester"}
{"id": "blended-math7233", "question": "Who teaches MATH 7233 Graph Theory, and what does that person research?", "stratum": "blended", "kind": "prose", "ground_truth": "", "expected_slugs": ["gabor-lippner", "course-math7233"], "source_facts": "COURSE: MATH 7233 Graph Theory\nDESCRIPTION: Covers fundamental concepts in graph theory. Topics include adjacency and incidence matrices, paths and connectedness, and vertex degrees and counting; trees and distance including properties of trees, distance in graphs, spanning trees, minimum spanning trees, and shortest paths; matchings and factors including matchings in bipartite graphs, Hall\u2019s matching condition, and min-max theorems; connectivity, such as vertex connectivity, edge connectivity, k-connected graphs, and Menger\u2019s theorem; \u2026\nTERM: Fall 2026 Semester\nINSTRUCTOR: Gabor Lippner\n\nINSTRUCTOR PROFILE:\nNAME: Gabor Lippner TITLE: COLLEGE: cos SLUG: gabor-lippner RESEARCH_INTERESTS: [\"random processes on networks\", \"differential equations on networks\", \"evolutionary biology\", \"quantum physics\", \"control theory\"] AREAS_OF_INTEREST: [\"analysis\", \"geometry\", \"combinatorics\"] BIOGRAPHY: Gabor Lippner received his PhD in Mathematics from E\u00f6tv\u00f6s Lor\u00e1nd University in Budapest, Hungary. Dr. Lippner combines tools from analysis, geometry, and combinatorics to study random processes and differential equations on networks, with applications spanning a broad spectrum from evolutionary biology through quantum physics to control theory.", "notes": "needs courses+people; MATH; term Fall 2026 Semester"}
{"id": "blended-biol4709", "question": "Who teaches BIOL 4709 Neurobiology of Learning and Memory, and what does that person research?", "stratum": "blended", "kind": "prose", "ground_truth": "", "expected_slugs": ["jamie-bunce", "course-biol4709"], "source_facts": "COURSE: BIOL 4709 Neurobiology of Learning and Memory\nDESCRIPTION: Explores the neurobiology of learning and memory from the level of the synapse up to the neural systems underlying emergent mnemonic function. Topics include the synaptic mechanisms underlying neural plasticity; the molecular basis of mnemonic processes; and the neural circuits serving distinct memory systems. In addition to lecture-based material, students utilize primary research and review articles from the current scientific literature to evaluate data and develop hypotheses via oral \u2026\nTERM: Fall 2026 Semester\nINSTRUCTOR: Jamie Bunce\n\nINSTRUCTOR PROFILE:\nNAME: Jamie Bunce TITLE: COLLEGE: cos SLUG: jamie-bunce RESEARCH_INTERESTS: [\"anxiety disorders\", \"post traumatic stress disorder\", \"prefrontal cortex\", \"medial temporal lobe\", \"synaptic interactions\", \"contextual memory\", \"emotional memory\", \"mnemonic information\"] BIOGRAPHY: Clinical findings and imaging studies in patients suffering from anxiety disorders, including post traumatic stress disorder, indicate that symptoms may manifest due to disruptions in pathways connecting the prefrontal cortex and the medial temporal lobe. These disruptions affect an individual's ability to accurately integrate context with emotionally salient memories. Dr. Jamie Bunce investigates these pathways and their synaptic interactions, which underlie the synthesis of contextual, emotional and mnemonic information, an understanding of which is prerequisite to the development of therapies for these psychiatric disorders.", "notes": "needs courses+people; BIOL; term Fall 2026 Semester"}
{"id": "blended-phys7323", "question": "Who teaches PHYS 7323 Elementary Particle Physics, and what does that person research?", "stratum": "blended", "kind": "prose", "ground_truth": "", "expected_slugs": ["tomasz-taylor", "course-phys7323"], "source_facts": "COURSE: PHYS 7323 Elementary Particle Physics\nDESCRIPTION: Presents a survey of the present state of elementary particle physics, suitable for all graduate students. Topics include overview of strong interactions and their connection to nuclear physics; nonrelativistic quark structure of strongly interacting particles (hadrons); color and the SU(3) Yang-Mills theory of strong interactions; coupling constant renormalization and asymptotic freedom; and the parton model of scattering. Covers weak interactions including phenomenology of the Fermi V-A \u2026\nTERM: Fall 2026 Semester\nINSTRUCTOR: Tomasz Taylor\n\nINSTRUCTOR PROFILE:\nNAME: Tomasz Taylor TITLE: Professor COLLEGE: cos SLUG: tomasz-taylor RESEARCH_INTERESTS: [\"superstring theory\", \"supersymmetric theories of fundamental interactions\", \"supergravity theories\", \"supersymmetry breaking\"] AREAS_OF_INTEREST: [\"couplings and masses\", \"large-distance macroscopic behaviour of superstring theory\", \"physical parameters from first principles\"] BIOGRAPHY: Professor Taylor's main research areas are superstring theory and in general, supersymmetric theories of fundamental interactions. In supersymmetric theories, half-integer spin particles, like quarks and leptons, are accompanied by spin zero particles with identical masses and charges. The advantage of supersymmetry is that it provides a natural framework for the existence of the Higgs particle which is responsible for electroweak symmetry breaking in the standard model of elementary particles. Moreover, quantum field theories based on local supersymmetry automatically incorporate gravity together with electroweak and strong forces. These supergravity theories suffer, though, from some \u2026", "notes": "needs courses+people; PHYS; term Fall 2026 Semester"}
{"id": "blended-chem5630", "question": "Who teaches CHEM 5630 Nucleic Acid Chemistry, and what does that person research?", "stratum": "blended", "kind": "prose", "ground_truth": "", "expected_slugs": ["ke-zhang", "course-chem5630"], "source_facts": "COURSE: CHEM 5630 Nucleic Acid Chemistry\nDESCRIPTION: Offers a broadband introduction to the field of nucleic acid chemistry. Nucleic acids are vital for biology, but their roles have been greatly expanded beyond storage of genetic information. The breadth of utility of nucleic acids stems from a precise understanding of their structures, modern means to synthesize and modify them, and the ability for nucleic acids to engage with varieties of enzymes/proteins and other synthetic/biological systems. Foundational topics include nucleic acid \u2026\nTERM: Fall 2026 Semester\nINSTRUCTOR: Ke Zhang\n\nINSTRUCTOR PROFILE:\nNAME: Ke Zhang TITLE: COLLEGE: cos SLUG: ke-zhang RESEARCH_INTERESTS: [\"polymer chemistry\", \"organic reactions\", \"colloidal syntheses\", \"bioconjugation techniques\", \"nanotechnology\"] AREAS_OF_INTEREST: [\"virus capsid mimics for vaccines\", \"nanoscopic drug carriers with triggered release\", \"polymer chemistry\", \"bionanotechnology\"] BIOGRAPHY: Research in the Zhang Lab is highly interdisciplinary. While their core research is polymer chemistry, they utilize a range of organic reactions, colloidal syntheses, and bioconjugation techniques to access their synthetic targets. Unlike those of an organic chemist, their targets are usually much larger, oftentimes large enough to be visualized by electronic or atomic microscopy (tens to hundreds of nanometers). Students and postdoctoral fellows will gain experience in organic and colloidal syntheses, polymer chemistry, and nanotechnology, while being exposed to interdisciplinary science. LABS_AND_GROUPS: [\"Zhang Lab\"] PROJECTS: [\"Virus capsid mimic for preventive or therapeutic vaccines\", \"Nanoscopic drug carrier that can release drug molecules at specific times or as an external trigger is applied\"]", "notes": "needs courses+people; CHEM; term Fall 2026 Semester"}
{"id": "blended-psyc6130", "question": "Who teaches PSYC 6130 Affective Computing, and what does that person research?", "stratum": "blended", "kind": "prose", "ground_truth": "", "expected_slugs": ["stacy-marsella", "course-psyc6130"], "source_facts": "COURSE: PSYC 6130 Affective Computing\nDESCRIPTION: Studies affective computing\u2014computing that relates to, arises from, or influences emotions. Offers an overview of the theory of human emotion (how it arises from and influences cognition, the body, and the social environment) and computational techniques for modeling human emotion processes as well as for recognizing and synthesizing emotional behavior. Discusses how these can be applied to application design. Offers students an opportunity to gain a strong background in the theory and practice \u2026\nTERM: Fall 2026 Semester\nINSTRUCTOR: Stacy Marsella\n\nINSTRUCTOR PROFILE:\nNAME: Stacy Marsella TITLE: COLLEGE: cos SLUG: stacy-marsella RESEARCH_INTERESTS: [\"computational modeling of human cognition\", \"emotion\", \"social behavior\", \"health interventions\", \"social skills training\", \"planning operations\", \"large-scale social simulations\", \"virtual humans\"] AREAS_OF_INTEREST: [\"agent research\", \"intelligent virtual agents\", \"affective computing\", \"experimental social psychology\"] BIOGRAPHY: Stacy Marsella's multidisciplinary research is grounded in the computational modeling of human cognition, emotion, and social behavior, as well as the evaluation of those models. Beyond its relevance to understanding human behavior, the work has applications in health interventions, social skills training, and planning operations. Marsella's applied work includes frameworks for large-scale social simulations of towns and a range of techniques and tools for creating virtual humans, facsimiles of people that can engage in face-to-face interactions. Prior to joining Northeastern, Marsella was a research professor at the University of Southern California (USC) and a research director at the \u2026 LABS_AND_GROUPS: [\"Institute for Creative Technologies\", \"USC Information Sciences Institute\"]", "notes": "needs courses+people; PSYC; term Fall 2026 Semester"}
{"id": "blended-binf6310", "question": "Who teaches BINF 6310 Introduction to Bioinformatics, and what does that person research?", "stratum": "blended", "kind": "prose", "ground_truth": "", "expected_slugs": ["andrej-savol", "course-binf6310"], "source_facts": "COURSE: BINF 6310 Introduction to Bioinformatics\nDESCRIPTION: Focuses on the core bioinformatics skill set and knowledge base necessary to conduct exploratory data analysis of large-scale biological data. Offers students an opportunity to work with the latest computational approaches in the context of real-world data and to obtain practical skills necessary to access, manage, and mine the vast biological information housed in public repositories. Presents core computational skills. Introduces DevOps concepts including version control, using Linux, as well \u2026\nTERM: Fall 2026 Semester\nINSTRUCTOR: Andrej Savol\n\nINSTRUCTOR PROFILE:\nNAME: Andrej Savol TITLE: Strategic Team Leader at Foundation Medicine, Inc. COLLEGE: cos SLUG: andrej-savol RESEARCH_INTERESTS: [\"precision oncology\", \"next generation sequencing data analysis\", \"biomarker development\", \"diagnostic methods\", \"multiomics\", \"RNA-seq\", \"DNA-seq\", \"ChIP-seq\", \"ATAC-seq\"] AREAS_OF_INTEREST: [\"precision diagnostics\", \"cellular behavior analysis\"] BIOGRAPHY: Andrej Savol, PhD is a computational biologist specializing in precision oncology and next generation sequencing data analysis. In industrial contexts his focus has been development of novel biomarkers and diagnostic methods that aid clinicians in treatment selection. He is currently a strategic team leader at Foundation Medicine, Inc., a precision diagnostics and informatics company. He completed his PhD in Computational Biology at the University of Pittsburgh and worked for several years at Massachusetts General Hospital in their Molecular Biology Department. LABS_AND_GROUPS: [\"Massachusetts General Hospital Molecular Biology Department\"]", "notes": "needs courses+people; BINF; term Fall 2026 Semester"}
{"id": "blended-eemb3460", "question": "Who teaches EEMB 3460 Conservation Biology, and what does that person research?", "stratum": "blended", "kind": "prose", "ground_truth": "", "expected_slugs": ["gabriela-garcia", "course-eemb3460"], "source_facts": "COURSE: EEMB 3460 Conservation Biology\nDESCRIPTION: Explores conservation biology, an interdisciplinary science that focuses on conservation of biological diversity at multiple levels. Emphasizes the causes and consequences of biodiversity loss and demonstrates how ecological and evolutionary principles are applied to conservation problems. Covers sustainability; climate change; introduced species; conservation of threatened and endangered species; and pollution, disease, and habitat restoration using examples from marine, aquatic, and \u2026\nTERM: Fall 2026 Semester\nINSTRUCTOR: Gabriela Garcia\n\nINSTRUCTOR PROFILE:\nNAME: Gabriela Garcia TITLE: COLLEGE: cos SLUG: gabriela-garcia RESEARCH_INTERESTS: [\"socio-ecological systems response to disturbance\", \"plant population and community dynamics\", \"relationships between plants and people\", \"human well-being and management decision-making\"] AREAS_OF_INTEREST: [\"biodiversity\", \"food security\", \"climate crises\", \"agroecosystem function\", \"ecological fieldwork\", \"qualitative interview analyses\", \"large-scale datasets\"] BIOGRAPHY: Research in Gabriela Garcia's group is positioned at the intersection of today's biodiversity, food security, and climate crises. As shifting precipitation patterns and extreme events disrupt agroecosystem function and threaten farmer livelihoods, they advance a predictive framework for understanding how socio-ecological systems respond to disturbance. The group integrates ecological fieldwork, qualitative interview analyses, and large-scale datasets to explore the reciprocal relationships between plants and people. They are especially interested in the ways plant population and community dynamics feedback with human well-being and management decision-making. They value cross-disciplinary, \u2026", "notes": "needs courses+people; EEMB; term Fall 2026 Semester"}
